WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.267 --> 00:00:07.140
"How to Control the Gastrointestinal Side Effects
of GLP-1 Drugs Like Ozempic (Semaglutide)"

00:00:07.140 --> 00:00:10.510
It appears the vast majority
of people prescribed GLP-1 drugs

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.113
stop taking them
in a matter of months.

00:00:13.113 --> 00:00:15.682
But compare that
to older weight loss drugs.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:19.152
Those had a 98 percent
failure rate.

00:00:19.152 --> 00:00:20.888
As I’ve mentioned,
in drug trials,

00:00:20.888 --> 00:00:22.990
most people taking
these types of weight-loss drugs

00:00:22.990 --> 00:00:25.492
started out obese
and ended up obese

00:00:25.492 --> 00:00:27.327
no matter how long
they stayed on them,

00:00:27.327 --> 00:00:28.896
but that’s just on average.

00:00:28.896 --> 00:00:32.699
Some people, despite being
on high-dose Ozempic for years,

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:35.202
actually ended up heavier
than when they started,

00:00:35.202 --> 00:00:37.237
and most remained overweight,

00:00:37.237 --> 00:00:39.573
but a small fraction of them,
about 1 in 25,

00:00:39.573 --> 00:00:43.443
plateaued down
at a normal weight.

00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:45.445
One in 25
doesn’t sound like a lot,

00:00:45.445 --> 00:00:47.781
but obese individuals
are rarely able

00:00:47.781 --> 00:00:49.616
to achieve a normal weight.

00:00:49.616 --> 00:00:52.219
Without something drastic,
like bariatric surgery,

00:00:52.219 --> 00:00:54.788
only about 1 in 200 men
with class 1 obesity,

00:00:54.788 --> 00:00:56.356
or 1 in 100 women,

00:00:56.356 --> 00:00:59.159
are able to find their way back
to a normal weight;

00:00:59.159 --> 00:01:02.296
so, these drugs
really can have a huge effect

00:01:02.296 --> 00:01:04.564
on a select few.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:07.034
Of course, you’d presumably
have to stay on them forever

00:01:07.034 --> 00:01:08.602
to maintain that weight loss

00:01:08.602 --> 00:01:11.838
at a cost of maybe $16,000
a year.

00:01:11.838 --> 00:01:14.241
But even for those
who can afford the cost,

00:01:14.241 --> 00:01:17.077
the main reason
they stop taking GLP-1 drugs

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:20.948
is the gastrointestinal
side-effect profile.

00:01:20.948 --> 00:01:23.550
The most common side effects
of Ozempic-type drugs

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:27.654
include nausea, vomiting,
diarrhea, and constipation.

00:01:27.654 --> 00:01:30.357
And they may also heighten
the risk of pancreatitis,

00:01:30.357 --> 00:01:31.959
kidney failure,
and thyroid cancer,

00:01:31.959 --> 00:01:34.861
but for now, let’s stick
to the common ones.

00:01:34.861 --> 00:01:37.731
These significant adverse
gastrointestinal effects

00:01:37.731 --> 00:01:40.701
were demonstrated
in virtually all trials,

00:01:40.701 --> 00:01:43.203
though they weren’t bad enough

00:01:43.203 --> 00:01:46.239
to cause more than 15 percent
of trial participants

00:01:46.239 --> 00:01:47.274
to drop out,

00:01:47.274 --> 00:01:49.810
and the vast majority
of adverse effects got better

00:01:49.810 --> 00:01:51.478
after they stopped the drugs.

00:01:51.478 --> 00:01:53.480
Here are the numbers:

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:55.682
44 percent
experienced nausea,

00:01:55.682 --> 00:01:57.250
30 percent diarrhea,

00:01:57.250 --> 00:01:58.385
24 percent vomiting,

00:01:58.385 --> 00:01:59.786
24 percent constipation,

00:01:59.786 --> 00:02:02.255
and 20 percent
abdominal pain.

00:02:02.255 --> 00:02:05.325
But 16 percent of those
on the placebo injections

00:02:05.325 --> 00:02:07.127
experienced nausea
or diarrhea too;

00:02:07.127 --> 00:02:08.862
so, it wasn’t all due
to the drug.

00:02:08.862 --> 00:02:11.965
People don’t tend to just
spontaneously vomit, though;

00:02:11.965 --> 00:02:15.235
so, that’s more likely
to be a drug-induced effect.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:18.338
So, some people may not
experience side effects at all,

00:02:18.338 --> 00:02:20.907
and these GI side effects
have been shown

00:02:20.907 --> 00:02:22.609
likely to be dose-dependent,

00:02:22.609 --> 00:02:26.079
and more common during
the dose escalation period.

00:02:26.079 --> 00:02:27.981
Thus, it’s recommended
that these medications

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:29.282
are started at lower doses

00:02:29.282 --> 00:02:31.251
and only increased
as tolerated.

00:02:31.251 --> 00:02:33.920
So, starting low
and going slow,

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.623
gradually nudging up the dose
over many months,

00:02:36.623 --> 00:02:39.559
can minimize
some of these side effects.

00:02:39.559 --> 00:02:42.229
And in terms
of what patients can do:

00:02:42.229 --> 00:02:44.798
Eat slowly,
smaller portions.

00:02:44.798 --> 00:02:46.400
Eat a low-fat diet.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:47.868
Small sips of fluid.

00:02:47.868 --> 00:02:49.236
Get some fresh air.

00:02:49.236 --> 00:02:51.938
Crackers, apples, mint, ginger.

00:02:51.938 --> 00:02:53.273
Stay hydrated.

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.476
Avoid sports drinks,
dairy products,

00:02:56.476 --> 00:02:59.046
coffee, alcohol,
and soft drinks.

00:02:59.046 --> 00:03:02.082
Temporarily reduce your intake
of fiber or increase it,

00:03:02.082 --> 00:03:03.450
depending on symptoms.

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.052
And if things get really bad,

00:03:05.052 --> 00:03:07.421
avoid drinking during meals.

00:03:07.421 --> 00:03:09.456
If the symptoms persist
or worsen,

00:03:09.456 --> 00:03:13.560
let your healthcare provider
know ASAP.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:17.030
One thing your doctor can do
is prescribe anti-vomiting drugs

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.866
to counter the side effects
of the original drugs,

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.802
but if you don’t feel nauseated
all the time,

00:03:21.802 --> 00:03:23.804
will you still lose weight?

00:03:23.804 --> 00:03:26.239
Yes, even people who don’t
feel sick to their stomach

00:03:26.239 --> 00:03:28.275
can experience
the appetite suppression

00:03:28.275 --> 00:03:30.377
that leads to weight loss.

00:03:30.377 --> 00:03:32.746
GLP-1 also makes you
feel fuller for longer

00:03:32.746 --> 00:03:36.016
by slowing down the rate at
which food leaves your stomach.

00:03:36.016 --> 00:03:38.652
That contributes
to the nausea and vomiting,

00:03:38.652 --> 00:03:40.821
and can lead
to a serious complication

00:03:40.821 --> 00:03:43.957
if you have to go under
anesthesia for surgery.

00:03:43.957 --> 00:03:47.594
GLP-1 drugs slow
your stomach down so much

00:03:47.594 --> 00:03:49.896
that the American Society
of Anesthesiologists

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:51.698
recommends people
stop taking the drugs

00:03:51.698 --> 00:03:54.434
for up to a week prior
to procedures

00:03:54.434 --> 00:03:57.003
so you don’t go under the knife
with a full stomach,

00:03:57.003 --> 00:03:59.473
for fear that you’ll aspirate
stomach contents

00:03:59.473 --> 00:04:01.274
into your lungs.

00:04:01.274 --> 00:04:03.977
It turns out that may not
even be long enough.

00:04:03.977 --> 00:04:06.179
Patients taking these drugs
had a higher prevalence

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:10.217
of increased residual
gastric contents despite fasting

00:04:10.217 --> 00:04:12.953
even if they stopped
these drugs a week before.

00:04:12.953 --> 00:04:14.554
So, if you’re going in
for elective surgery,

00:04:14.554 --> 00:04:16.289
consider stopping a drug
like Ozempic

00:04:16.289 --> 00:04:18.825
at least three weeks before.

00:04:18.825 --> 00:04:20.760
Stopping these drugs
for a month or so

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:22.729
may not be tenable
for diabetics

00:04:22.729 --> 00:04:24.431
using it to control
their blood sugars,

00:04:24.431 --> 00:04:26.500
but if you’re just doing it
for your weight,

00:04:26.500 --> 00:04:28.435
it’s a good idea to hold off.

